Here are the Git commands most developers use every day, in simple words:

* git init — Start a new Git project.
* git clone — Copy a project from GitHub.
* git status — Check what changed.
* git add . — Add all changes.
* git add <file> — Add one file.
* git commit -m "message" — Save your changes.
* git push — Upload changes to GitHub.
* git pull — Download the latest changes.
* git fetch — Check for updates.
* git branch — Show all branches.
* git branch <name> — Create a new branch.
* git switch <name> — Switch to another branch.
* git switch -c <name> — Create and switch to a new branch.
* git merge <branch> — Combine two branches.
* git rebase <branch> — Move your changes onto the latest branch.
* git log — View commit history.
* git log --oneline — View short commit history.
* git diff — See what changed.
* git stash — Save unfinished work temporarily.
* git stash pop — Restore unfinished work.
* git restore <file> — Undo changes to a file.
* git rm <file> — Delete a tracked file.
* git mv <old> <new> — Rename or move a file.
* git remote -v — Show GitHub connection.
* git remote add origin <URL> — Connect to GitHub.
* git tag <name> — Create a version tag.
* git reset --soft HEAD~1 — Undo the last commit but keep the changes.
* git reset --hard HEAD — Discard all uncommitted changes.
* git checkout <branch> — Switch branches (older command still widely used).
1-everday use git commands
	•	git fetch — get updates without merging
	•	git log — view commit history
	•	git diff — see line-by-line changes

Undoing things

	•	git reset <file> — unstage a file
	•	git checkout -- <file> — discard local changes to a file
	•	git revert <commit> — undo a commit by creating a new one

VS Code-specific tips

	•	Source Control panel (Ctrl+Shift+G / Cmd+Shift+G) does staging, committing, and pushing without touching the terminal
	•	The GitLens extension is worth installing — adds blame annotations, history, and comparisons right in the editor
	•	VS Code shows inline diff gutters (green/red bars) for uncommitted changes automatically

